---
// "How the tiers and power rankings work": which ranking sets the tiers, how each of
// Jason's four power rankings is built from the box score and NBA.com defense, and why.
import { TIER_EMOJI } from "../lib/tiers";
import { HEAT_NAME, MODEL_NAME, TIER_SOURCE_NAME, leagueModel, tierInfo } from "../lib/players";

interface Props { open?: boolean }
const { open = false } = Astro.props;
const [info, league] = await Promise.all([tierInfo(), leagueModel()]);
const snap = info.snapshot;
const fmtDate = (d: string) => new Date(d + "T12:00:00").toLocaleDateString("en-US", { dateStyle: "medium" });
const builtFrom = snap ? TIER_SOURCE_NAME[snap.source] ?? snap.source : null;
const chosen = info.ranking ? TIER_SOURCE_NAME[info.ranking] ?? info.ranking : null;
---
<details class="explain" id="how" open={open}>
  <summary>How the tiers and power rankings work</summary>
  <div class="stack">
    <h3>The tier list</h3>
    <p>Tiers are set by the commissioner's power ranking{chosen ? <>: <b>{chosen}</b></> : " (which of the four is being picked now)"}.
      Ranks 1–{info.t1} are {TIER_EMOJI.T1} Tier 1 and ranks {info.t1 + 1}–{info.t2} are {TIER_EMOJI.T2} Tier 2: a team can roster at most
      {" "}{info.cap1} of each. Everyone else is {TIER_EMOJI.T3} Tier 3, no limit. Players on IR don't count.
      The list is frozen as a numbered snapshot and refreshed every {info.weeks} weeks, so tiers don't jump around between refreshes.</p>
    {snap && (
      <p class="small muted">Current snapshot: version {snap.version}, in effect since {fmtDate(snap.effective_date)}, built from {builtFrom}
        {snap.run_date ? <> (the {fmtDate(snap.run_date)} run)</> : ""}.
        {snap.source === "espn" && " The first power-ranking snapshot replaces it once the model has run on this season's games."}</p>
    )}
    <p>The tier caps (3 T1, 3 T2) are a guideline: the site notes each team's tier counts, and nothing is enforced.</p>

    <h3>Weekly Power (NBA model)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Blocks of 5 games.</b> Each NBA team's schedule is cut into blocks of 5 games. Inside a block the newest game counts 10, then 9, 8, 7, 6.
        Blocks count 21 for the newest, then 20, 19, 18 and so on, never below 16. Recent form matters most, but a hot week can't erase a season.</li>
      <li><b>Game Score</b> is the box-score base (John Hollinger's formula): PTS + 0.4·FGM − 0.7·FGA − 0.4·(FTA − FTM) + 0.7·OREB + 0.3·DREB + STL + 0.7·AST + 0.7·BLK − 0.4·PF − TOV.</li>
      <li><b>Defense fantasy can't see.</b> Seven NBA.com tracking stats: deflections, contested shots, charges drawn, box-outs, loose balls,
        and defended FG% and rim FG% (counted as points saved against the league average, so a defender who faced one shot can't game it).
        Each is compared to every player with 3+ games in the block (a z-score), the seven are averaged, and one standard deviation is worth 2 Game Score points.</li>
      <li><b>Close games count more.</b> Box-score plays made with the score within 3 get +5%, within 4–6 +3%, within 7–9 +1%. That cuts both ways: a clutch turnover costs more.</li>
      <li>A DNP or a game under 6 minutes takes its slot but isn't averaged in. A block with no real games is skipped.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Minutes Power (NBA model)</h3>
    <p>The same ingredients per 48 minutes, from each block's totals. A block counts only if the player logged 30+ real minutes in it, so
      matchup and bench players who produce in short minutes get ranked too. Defense is compared on per-48 rates.</p>
    <h3>Fantasy-scoring versions</h3>
    <p>{HEAT_NAME.weekly_fantasy} and {HEAT_NAME.minutes_fantasy} use the same blocks, weights and close-game boost, but swap Game Score for
      league fantasy points ({MODEL_NAME[league]} now, including the team-win bonus) and leave the defense numbers out. They answer "who's scoring
      the most fantasy points lately", while the NBA versions answer "who's actually playing the best".</p>
    <h3>League points</h3>
    <p>Plain season fantasy points under the scoring model you pick. They're totals, so they reward games played and health as well as quality.</p>
    <h3>Movement</h3>
    <p>+/- shows how many places a player moved (+3 = up three places, -2 = down two) since the last run of that ranking (or the last tier snapshot), and the score change
      shows how much his power score rose or fell. NEW means he wasn't in the previous list. Each power ranking keeps its top 100.</p>
  </div>
</details>
